        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1016,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Holds a set of points and finds, for any point you ask about, the nearest of them - or the nearest few - and how far away they are.\n\nIt sorts the points into a tree once, when they arrive, so that each question after that is answered without measuring the distance to every point: thousands of points cost little more than ten. Use it to find which performer, marker, zone centre or light is closest to a hand; the nearest pose in a library of poses (each pose a point with many coordinates); the nearest neighbours in a point cloud.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.kdtree <k: int>\nDefault: k 1 (the single nearest).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.kdtree 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nquery:\nThe point to look near - a list of coordinates - or several, as rows. Every input is looked up and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\npoints:\nThe set of points, as rows of coordinates (x y, or x y z, or as many as you like). Held;\nthe tree is rebuilt only when a new set arrives.\n\nk (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many nearest points to give, nearest first.\n\nmax distance (float box, also an inlet):\nIgnore points further away than this. 0 (the default) means no limit. Where nothing is within reach, the index is -1 and the distance inf.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nindices:\nWhich of the points is nearest - its row number - or, for k above 1, the nearest k.\n\ndistances:\nHow far away each is. Sent first, then the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour points at the corners of a square, numbered 0 to 3: -1 -1, 1 -1, 1 1, -1 1. A point circles round the middle (left), and the plot shows which corner it is nearest to: 0, 1, 2, 3 in turn as it goes round, and the distance to it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.distance_from_target and proximity_trigger measure the distance to a few targets. t.cdist gives every distance between two sets. hypot and np.linalg.norm measure one.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Holds a set of points and finds, for any point you ask about, the nearest of them - or the nearest few - and how far away they are.\n\nIt sorts the points into a tree once, when they arrive, so that each question after that is answered without measuring the distance to every point: thousands of points cost little more than ten. Use it to find which performer, marker, zone centre or light is closest to a hand; the nearest pose in a library of poses (each pose a point with many coordinates); the nearest neighbours in a point cloud.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.kdtree <k: int>\nDefault: k 1 (the single nearest).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nscipy.kdtree 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nquery:\nThe point to look near - a list of coordinates - or several, as rows. Every input is looked up and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\npoints:\nThe set of points, as rows of coordinates (x y, or x y z, or as many as you like). Held;\nthe tree is rebuilt only when a new set arrives.\n\nk (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many nearest points to give, nearest first.\n\nmax distance (float box, also an inlet):\nIgnore points further away than this. 0 (the default) means no limit. Where nothing is within reach, the index is -1 and the distance inf.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nindices:\nWhich of the points is nearest - its row number - or, for k above 1, the nearest k.\n\ndistances:\nHow far away each is. Sent first, then the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour points at the corners of a square, numbered 0 to 3: -1 -1, 1 -1, 1 1, -1 1. A point circles round the middle (left), and the plot shows which corner it is nearest to: 0, 1, 2, 3 in turn as it goes round, and the distance to it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.distance_from_target and proximity_trigger measure the distance to a few targets. t.cdist gives every distance between two sets. hypot and np.linalg.norm measure one."
        },
